Let f:R² - {(0, 0)} -> R be defined by 

		f(x, y) = (x² - y²)/(x² + y²)

Let's see what happens to f as x = (x, y) approaches 0 = (0, 0). 
(Note that f is underfined at the origin, although this is of no consequence insofar as evaluating limits is concerned)
Along the  x-axis (i.e., the line y = 0), we calculate the value of f to be 

		f(x, 0) = (x² - 0)/(x² + 0) = 1

Thus, as x approahces 0 along the line y = 0, the values of f remain constant, and so 

		lim(sub(x -> 0 along y = 0)) f(x) = 1. 

Along the y-axis, however, the value of f is 

		f(0, y) = (0 - y²)/(0 + y²) = -1

Hence, 

		lim(sub(x -> 0 along x = 0)) f(x) = -1

Indeed, the value of f is constant along each line through the origin. 
Along the line y = mx,m constant, we have

		f(x, mx) = (x² - m²x²)/(x² + m²x²) = (x²(1 - m²))/(x²(1 + m²)) = (1 - m²)/(1 + m²)

Therefore, 

		lim(sub(x -> 0 along y = mx)) f(x) = (1 - m²)/(1 + m²)

As a result, the limit of f as x appraches 0 does not exist, since f has diffeent "limiting values" depending on which direction we approach the origin. 
(see Figure 2.39)
That is, no matter how close we come to the origin, we can find points x such that f(x) is not near any numebr L ∈ R. 
(In other words, every open disk centered at (0,0), not matter how small, is mapped onto the interval [-1, 1])
If we graph the surface having equation 

		z = (x² - y²)/(x² + y²)

(Figure 2.40), we can see quite clearly that there is no limiting value as x approahces the origin

